Fillets: roundings joined across a rib or a notch whose sides are square
to the spine, and joins chained over several stripes (mitcad#343).

A pocket's ceiling rounded with its wall (radius 8), the wall's edge broken
by ribs 1 thick standing out 6 from the wall: the edge chain stops at each
rib, and the bodies stored in the files have one cylinder over the whole
wall, cut by the ribs. ChFi3d_JoinAcrossNotches (0241) joins the ends on
either side of a rib: one stripe's blend runs on over the gap, cut by the
rib's faces in turn. The rib's sides cut the blend along sections, so they
give no piece, and the piece on the rib's end face starts where the blend
crosses that face's far edge, not at the other stripe's end point. The
stripes then met at that end point while the first piece started
elsewhere: the piece's edge on the rib's end ran from the other stripe's
point, the rib's side lost its edge with the blend, and the shell was open.
Now that piece is split at its middle (ChFi3d_SplitPieceAtSection), its
half at the other stripe's end goes to that stripe, after its end piece,
and the stripes meet at the split, a whole section on one face; the part
of the end section on the rib's side is that face's edge with the blend,
which ChFi3d_FilDS makes between two pieces of one stripe (PartSection).
Only where the blends are one surface, with the same sides, and the
stripes run the same way at the gap; elsewhere the join is as before.

Where a stripe joined to another before is joined again (three edges, two
ribs), the join gave the other stripe's surface only to the SurfData of
the stripe it extends, so the one joined before kept the old surface and
was a face of its own with no edge at the section between them. Now every
SurfData on that surface, in any stripe, takes the new one.

Upstream: new.
